    sections: [
      { title: 'Geological Formation & Pegmatite Systems', content: 'The Ghazni lithium pegmatites formed during the Himalayan orogeny approximately 25 million years ago through complex geological processes involving the crystallization of lithium-rich granitic magmas in the deep crust. The pegmatites formed in favorable structural zones including fractures, faults, and fold structures, where slow cooling allowed large crystals to develop over millions of years, creating spodumene crystals reaching several meters in length and exceptional lithium grades. The pegmatite bodies occur in swarms across a 200-kilometer belt, with over 1,000 individual pegmatites identified.\nThe largest bodies reach 50 meters in width and extend for several kilometers along strike, containing lithium grades consistently above 2% Li2O with peak grades of 3.5% Li2O in core zones.' },
      { title: 'Battery-Grade Processing Technology', content: 'Converting spodumene ore to battery-grade lithium requires sophisticated processing technology adapted to pegmatite ore characteristics. The processing sequence includes mining and crushing of pegmatite ore, dense media separation producing 6% Li2O concentrate, calcination at 1,050°C converting alpha-spodumene to reactive beta-spodumene, sulfuric acid roasting liberating lithium, and purification producing 99.5% lithium carbonate or 99.9% lithium hydroxide meeting battery specifications. The proposed Ghazni Lithium Complex would process 10 million tons of ore annually, producing 500,000 tons of battery-grade lithium chemicals—equivalent to 25% of current global production. By-product recovery of tantalum, tin, and beryllium adds $2 billion in additional annual revenue.' },
      { title: 'Electric Vehicle Revolution & Global Supply', content: 'Afghanistan\'s lithium will power the global transition to electric transportation, with production sufficient to supply batteries for hundreds of millions of vehicles. A single electric vehicle battery contains 8-12 kilograms of lithium; Afghanistan\'s reserves could therefore supply batteries for 10-15 billion vehicles over the deposit\'s productive life. Strategic partnerships with EV manufacturers and battery producers would ensure stable off-take agreements while technology transfer builds domestic manufacturing capacity. A Tesla Gigafactory-scale battery plant in Afghanistan could produce 500,000 battery packs annually, creating 20,000 manufacturing jobs.\nThe geopolitical implications are profound: Afghanistan\'s lithium provides Western nations and their allies an alternative to Chinese-dominated lithium supply chains.' },
      { title: 'Research & Development Hub', content: 'The Ghazni Lithium Institute would become a global center for lithium research, battery technology, and sustainable mining practices. Research priorities include development of more efficient lithium extraction processes, battery recycling and circular economy solutions, new battery chemistries utilizing Afghan minerals, and environmental rehabilitation techniques for pegmatite mining. Partnerships with MIT, Stanford, and international mining universities would attract world-class researchers while training Afghan scientists and engineers. Annual research investment of $200 million—funded by lithium revenues—would position Afghanistan at the forefront of clean energy technology.' },
      { title: 'Infrastructure Development & Logistics', content: 'Developing Ghazni\'s lithium operations requires innovative infrastructure solutions adapted to remote terrain and high-altitude conditions. Infrastructure development includes road infrastructure with 300 kilometers of paved access roads, power infrastructure including connection to regional grids providing 500 MW capacity, water infrastructure utilizing regional sources with treatment facilities, and telecommunications infrastructure providing high-speed internet and mobile coverage. The comprehensive infrastructure development creates 3,000 construction jobs over 3 years, with ongoing operations employing 5,000 workers permanently.' },
      { title: 'Economic Impact & Regional Development', content: 'Ghazni lithium development represents a transformational opportunity for regional economic development. At full production, the lithium operations will generate $15 billion annually in revenue from lithium sales, $3 billion in royalties and taxes to the Afghan government, $500 million in wages to Afghan workers, and $2 billion in procurement from local suppliers. Over the 50-year development horizon, cumulative benefits will include $750 billion in total revenue, $150 billion in government revenue, 5 million person-years of employment, and development of ancillary industries. The project\'s economic multiplier effect—estimated at 4.5x—means that every dollar of direct revenue generates $4.50 in total economic activity.' },
      { title: 'Environmental Management & Sustainability', content: 'Sustainable lithium mining in Ghazni\'s pristine mountain environment requires comprehensive environmental management addressing impacts on water, air, land, and ecosystems. The operations incorporate advanced environmental controls including water treatment systems protecting mountain streams, air quality management minimizing dust and emissions, land rehabilitation restoring mined areas, and biodiversity conservation protecting alpine ecosystems. Tailings management employs modern filtered tailings technology, reducing water consumption and environmental risk. The operations\' closure plan, funded by a dedicated trust fund, ensures that mining areas will be fully rehabilitated within 10 years of operations ceasing.' },
      { title: 'Workforce Development & Skills Training', content: 'The lithium project includes comprehensive workforce development programs creating opportunities for Afghan workers at all skill levels. Employment targets ensure that 90% of operational workers are Afghan nationals, with priority given to local communities. Training programs develop skills in mining, processing, chemistry, and management, creating career pathways from entry-level positions to technical and leadership roles. The project\'s training center provides classroom and hands-on training, with partnerships with international companies enabling knowledge transfer.' },
      { title: 'International Partnerships & Collaboration', content: 'Strategic international partnerships enable knowledge transfer, technology access, and market development. The project\'s partnership strategy includes joint ventures with international lithium processing companies, technology partnerships with equipment suppliers, research collaborations with universities, and customer relationships with major battery manufacturers. Partnerships enable access to world-class technology and expertise, supporting operations while building domestic capacity.' },
      { title: 'Market Dynamics & Global Supply Chain', content: 'Ghazni\'s lithium production will significantly impact global markets, with annual production of 500,000 tons representing 25% of current global supply. The mine\'s location provides competitive advantages including proximity to regional markets, access to growing demand in Asia and Europe, and potential for value-added processing serving regional manufacturing. Market analysis indicates strong long-term demand fundamentals, with lithium essential for electric vehicles, renewable energy storage, and consumer electronics. Long-term supply agreements with major consumers provide revenue security while ensuring market access.' },
      { title: 'Resource Expansion Potential', content: 'Comprehensive exploration programs have identified exceptional potential for resource expansion, with geological indicators suggesting potential for 300+ million tons LCE across the broader belt system. Exploration targets extend across thousands of square kilometers of prospective terrain, representing substantial upside potential. Modern exploration techniques including hyperspectral remote sensing, geophysical surveys, and deep core drilling programs will systematically test these targets. The comprehensive geological database incorporating over 75,000 meters of drilling data, 3,500 geochemical samples, and extensive remote sensing surveys creates a robust foundation for resource expansion.' },
      { title: 'Technological Innovation & Automation', content: 'The lithium operations incorporate cutting-edge technology and automation systems positioning it as a leader in sustainable mining practices. Advanced process control systems utilizing artificial intelligence optimize processing operations in real-time, maximizing recovery while minimizing energy consumption and environmental impact. Digital twin technology creates virtual replicas of processing operations, enabling simulation and optimization before implementing changes. Predictive maintenance systems utilize sensors and machine learning to predict equipment failures before they occur, reducing downtime and maintenance costs.' }
    ]
