    sections: [
      { title: 'Geological Formation & Metamorphism', content: 'Afghanistan\'s marble deposits formed through complex metamorphic processes that transformed ancient limestone deposits into fine-grained marble over millions of years. The original limestone deposits accumulated in ancient marine environments during the Paleozoic and Mesozoic eras, approximately 200-500 million years ago, through the accumulation of calcium carbonate from marine organisms. These limestone deposits were subsequently buried and subjected to heat and pressure during mountain-building events, transforming the limestone through recrystallization into fine-grained marble with exceptional purity and structural integrity.\nThe marble deposits occur in massive formations extending over hundreds of square kilometers, with individual quarries containing millions of cubic meters of extractable stone.' },
      { title: 'Premium Quality Characteristics', content: 'Afghan white marble exhibits characteristics that place it among the world\'s finest architectural stones: exceptional whiteness achieving 94+ on the Hunter L scale, high translucency allowing light penetration to 15mm, minimal veining ensuring uniform appearance, and excellent polishing characteristics achieving mirror finish. Comparative testing by international laboratories confirms Afghan marble matches or exceeds Carrara specifications for purity, strength, and appearance. The material is suitable for monumental sculptures, luxury building facades, premium floor and wall tiles, and high-end interior design. Additional marble varieties including onyx, travertine, and colored marbles expand the product range to serve diverse market segments.' },
      { title: 'Modern Quarrying Technology', content: 'Developing Afghanistan\'s marble potential requires modernization of quarrying and processing operations utilizing world-class technology. Diamond wire cutting—the global standard for premium marble—minimizes waste and damage while producing consistent blocks for processing, achieving recovery rates exceeding 40% compared to traditional methods. The proposed Herat Marble Complex would include mechanized quarrying with diamond wire saws cutting blocks up to 3 meters in length, gang saw processing for slabs and tiles, automated polishing and finishing lines achieving mirror finish, and comprehensive logistics infrastructure for export. Annual production capacity of 1 million tons would generate $500 million in export revenue while directly employing 5,000 workers.' },
      { title: 'Processing & Value Addition', content: 'Converting raw marble blocks into finished products requires sophisticated processing technology maximizing value while meeting market specifications. The processing sequence includes block cutting utilizing diamond wire saws producing slabs of consistent thickness, gang saw processing creating tiles and slabs meeting dimensional specifications, automated polishing achieving mirror finish with consistent quality, edge finishing creating precise edges for installation, and quality control ensuring products meet specifications. Finished products including polished slabs, tiles, countertops, and custom-cut pieces command premium prices in international markets, with value addition creating 5-10x higher prices than raw blocks.' },
      { title: 'Market Dynamics & Global Demand', content: 'Afghanistan\'s marble production will contribute to global markets, with annual production of 1 million tons representing significant supply. The mine\'s location provides competitive advantages including proximity to regional markets in Central Asia, Middle East, and South Asia, access to growing demand in construction and infrastructure development, and potential for value-added processing serving regional manufacturing. Market analysis indicates strong long-term demand fundamentals, with marble essential for luxury construction, infrastructure development, and interior design applications. Long-term supply agreements with major consumers provide revenue security while ensuring market access.' },
      { title: 'Economic Impact & Regional Development', content: 'Afghanistan marble development represents a transformational opportunity for regional economic development. At full production, the marble operations will generate $500 million annually in revenue from marble sales, $100 million in royalties and taxes to the Afghan government, $75 million in wages to Afghan workers, and $150 million in procurement from local suppliers. Over the 50-year development horizon, cumulative benefits will include $25 billion in total revenue, $5 billion in government revenue, 1.25 million person-years of employment, and development of ancillary industries. The project\'s economic multiplier effect—estimated at 3.8x—means that every dollar of direct revenue generates $3.80 in total economic activity.' },
      { title: 'Environmental Management & Sustainability', content: 'Sustainable marble quarrying requires comprehensive environmental management addressing impacts on water, air, land, and ecosystems. The operations incorporate advanced environmental controls including water treatment systems protecting water quality, air quality management minimizing dust and emissions, land rehabilitation restoring quarried areas, and biodiversity conservation protecting ecosystems. Waste management programs utilize marble waste for aggregate production, reducing waste while creating additional value. The operations\' closure plan, funded by a dedicated trust fund, ensures that quarried areas will be fully rehabilitated within 10 years of operations ceasing.' },
      { title: 'Workforce Development & Skills Training', content: 'The marble project includes comprehensive workforce development programs creating opportunities for Afghan workers at all skill levels. Employment targets ensure that 90% of operational workers are Afghan nationals, with priority given to local communities. Training programs develop skills in quarrying, processing, finishing, and management, creating career pathways from entry-level positions to technical and leadership roles. The project\'s training center provides classroom and hands-on training, with partnerships with international companies enabling knowledge transfer.' },
      { title: 'Infrastructure Development', content: 'Developing Afghanistan\'s marble operations requires strategic infrastructure investment supporting quarrying, processing, and export activities. Road infrastructure includes 200 kilometers of paved access roads connecting quarries to processing facilities and export terminals. Rail infrastructure includes connections to regional rail networks enabling efficient marble transport. Power infrastructure includes connection to regional grids and dedicated power generation facilities. Water infrastructure includes pipelines from regional sources, treatment facilities, and storage reservoirs ensuring reliable water supply. Telecommunications infrastructure provides high-speed internet and mobile coverage.' },
      { title: 'International Partnerships & Collaboration', content: 'Strategic international partnerships enable knowledge transfer, technology access, and market development. The project\'s partnership strategy includes joint ventures with international stone companies, technology partnerships with equipment suppliers, research collaborations with universities, and customer relationships with major construction companies and architectural firms. Partnerships enable access to world-class technology and expertise, supporting operations while building domestic capacity. Knowledge transfer programs ensure that Afghan workers and companies benefit from international expertise.' },
      { title: 'Historical Heritage & Cultural Significance', content: 'Afghanistan\'s marble has been used in some of the world\'s most prestigious architectural projects, including the Taj Mahal and numerous mosques throughout the Islamic world, attesting to its centuries-long reputation for excellence. This historical heritage provides a foundation for modern development, with the marble\'s proven quality and durability supporting its use in contemporary applications. The cultural significance of Afghan marble extends beyond its physical properties, representing a connection to centuries of architectural tradition and craftsmanship.' },
      { title: 'Resource Expansion Potential', content: 'Comprehensive exploration programs have identified exceptional potential for resource expansion, with geological indicators suggesting potential for 10+ billion cubic meters of marble resources across the broader region. Exploration targets extend across thousands of square kilometers of prospective terrain, representing substantial upside potential. Modern exploration techniques including geological mapping, core drilling, and detailed analysis will systematically test these targets. The comprehensive geological database incorporating over 50,000 meters of drilling data, 2,500 petrographic samples, and extensive geological mapping creates a robust foundation for resource expansion.' }
    ]
