    sections: [
      { title: 'Geological Formation & Coal Genesis', content: 'Afghanistan\'s coal deposits formed during the Carboniferous and Permian periods approximately 300-350 million years ago through complex geological processes involving the burial and transformation of ancient swamp forests. These forests, dominated by primitive plants including tree ferns, horsetails, and early conifers, accumulated in vast peat swamps that were subsequently buried by sedimentary deposits. Over millions of years, heat and pressure from overlying rocks transformed the organic material through stages of coalification: first to lignite (brown coal), then to bituminous coal, and finally to anthracite (hard coal) in areas of highest metamorphism.\nThe coal deposits occur in 35 major basins across northern Afghanistan, with individual basins containing multiple coal seams extending over hundreds of square kilometers.' },
      { title: 'Coal Types & Quality Spectrum', content: 'Afghan coal deposits span the complete quality spectrum from low-rank lignite to high-rank anthracite, with significant resources of premium metallurgical coal suitable for steel production. The Ishpushta deposits in Baghlan Province produce exceptional coking coal with 73% fixed carbon, low ash content (8%), low sulfur (0.5%), and excellent coking properties meeting the strictest specifications for integrated steel mills. This premium metallurgical coal commands premium prices exceeding $200 per ton in international markets. Bituminous thermal coal resources provide essential fuel for domestic heating and industrial steam generation.\nModern clean coal power plants utilizing advanced combustion technology could utilize these resources while minimizing environmental impact.' },
      { title: 'Underground Mining Operations', content: 'Most Afghan coal occurs in underground seams requiring shaft or adit mining—more labor-intensive than open-pit operations but with significantly lower environmental impact. Modern longwall mining techniques could achieve high productivity while ensuring worker safety through comprehensive safety systems, automated equipment, and continuous monitoring. The proposed Baghlan Coal Mine would employ longwall mining for main seams utilizing automated shearers and hydraulic roof supports, continuous miners for development headings, conveyor transport systems moving coal from face to surface efficiently, and coal preparation plants for beneficiation. Annual production of 5 million tons would support 5,000 direct mining jobs plus 15,000 indirect positions in support services.' },
      { title: 'Clean Coal Technologies & Environmental Management', content: 'Sustainable coal mining requires comprehensive environmental management addressing impacts on water, air, land, and climate. The operations incorporate advanced clean coal technologies including supercritical and ultra-supercritical power plants achieving 45-50% thermal efficiency, integrated gasification combined cycle (IGCC) systems enabling carbon capture, flue gas desulfurization removing sulfur dioxide emissions, selective catalytic reduction removing nitrogen oxides, and carbon capture and storage (CCS) systems capturing CO2 for sequestration or utilization.\nEnvironmental controls include water treatment systems protecting water quality, air quality management minimizing dust and emissions, land rehabilitation restoring mined areas, and biodiversity conservation protecting ecosystems.' },
      { title: 'Power Generation & Energy Security', content: 'Afghanistan\'s coal resources provide essential feedstock for power generation, supporting energy security while enabling economic development. Modern coal-fired power plants utilizing clean coal technologies could generate 2,000 MW of electricity, meeting a significant portion of Afghanistan\'s power needs while reducing dependence on imported energy. The power generation facilities would employ supercritical technology achieving 45% thermal efficiency, flue gas desulfurization removing 95% of sulfur emissions, selective catalytic reduction removing 90% of nitrogen oxides, and carbon capture systems capturing 90% of CO2 emissions.\nEnergy security benefits include reduced dependence on imported fuels, lower electricity costs supporting industrial development, and reliable power supply enabling economic growth.' },
      { title: 'Steel Production & Metallurgical Coal', content: 'Premium metallurgical coal from Afghanistan\'s coal basins provides essential feedstock for steel production, supporting industrial development while enabling export markets. The premium coking coal with 73% fixed carbon, low ash, and low sulfur meets specifications for integrated steel mills producing high-quality steel products. Steel production utilizing Afghan metallurgical coal would support regional manufacturing, infrastructure development, and export markets. The metallurgical coal\'s exceptional quality commands premium prices exceeding $200 per ton in international markets, maximizing economic value while supporting steel industry development.' },
      { title: 'Economic Impact & Regional Development', content: 'Afghanistan coal development represents a transformational opportunity for northern provinces. At full production, the coal operations will generate $800 million annually in revenue from coal sales, $150 million in royalties and taxes to the Afghan government, $100 million in wages to Afghan workers, and $250 million in procurement from local suppliers. Over the 40-year development horizon, cumulative benefits will include $32 billion in total revenue, $6 billion in government revenue, 1.2 million person-years of employment, and development of ancillary industries. The project\'s economic multiplier effect—estimated at 3.5x—means that every dollar of direct revenue generates $3.50 in total economic activity.' },
      { title: 'Workforce Development & Skills Training', content: 'The coal project includes comprehensive workforce development programs creating opportunities for Afghan workers at all skill levels. Employment targets ensure that 90% of operational workers are Afghan nationals, with priority given to local communities. Training programs develop skills in mining, processing, maintenance, and management, creating career pathways from entry-level positions to technical and leadership roles. Comprehensive training programs would develop skilled miners meeting international safety certifications, with ongoing education ensuring continuous improvement in safety and productivity.' },
      { title: 'Infrastructure Development', content: 'Developing Afghanistan\'s coal operations requires strategic infrastructure investment supporting mining, processing, and export activities. Road infrastructure includes 200 kilometers of paved access roads connecting mines to processing facilities and export terminals. Rail infrastructure includes connections to regional rail networks enabling efficient coal transport. Power infrastructure includes connection to regional grids and dedicated power generation facilities. Water infrastructure includes pipelines from regional sources, treatment facilities, and storage reservoirs ensuring reliable water supply. Telecommunications infrastructure provides high-speed internet and mobile coverage, enabling remote operations monitoring.' },
      { title: 'Market Dynamics & Global Supply', content: 'Afghanistan\'s coal production will contribute to regional and global markets, with annual production of 10 million tons representing significant supply. The mine\'s location provides competitive advantages including proximity to regional markets, access to growing demand in Pakistan, India, and Central Asia, and potential for value-added processing serving regional manufacturing. Market analysis indicates strong long-term demand fundamentals for both metallurgical and thermal coal, with metallurgical coal essential for steel production and thermal coal important for power generation. Long-term supply agreements with major consumers provide revenue security while ensuring market access.' },
      { title: 'International Partnerships & Collaboration', content: 'Strategic international partnerships enable knowledge transfer, technology access, and market development. The project\'s partnership strategy includes joint ventures with international mining and power companies, technology partnerships with equipment suppliers, research collaborations with universities, and customer relationships with major steel producers and power utilities. Partnerships enable access to world-class technology and expertise, supporting operations while building domestic capacity. Knowledge transfer programs ensure that Afghan workers and companies benefit from international expertise.' },
      { title: 'Resource Expansion Potential', content: 'Comprehensive exploration programs have identified exceptional potential for resource expansion, with geological indicators suggesting potential for 6+ billion tons of coal resources across the broader basin system. Exploration targets extend across thousands of square kilometers of prospective terrain, representing substantial upside potential. Modern exploration techniques including seismic surveys, core drilling, and detailed geological mapping will systematically test these targets. The comprehensive geological database incorporating over 100,000 meters of drilling data, 5,000 geochemical samples, and extensive geophysical surveys creates a robust foundation for resource expansion.' }
    ]
